7. Regulatory Context & Label Requirements
The application must validate distilled spirits labels against current U.S. Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau (TTB) requirements. The purpose of this validation is to determine whether a submitted label would likely pass or fail compliance review before manual inspection. The system should not simply detect text; it must evaluate formatting, wording, placement, and required regulatory elements based on TTB labeling standards.
Required Label Elements
The system must verify the presence and correctness of the following mandatory label components:
Brand Name
Class/Type Designation
Alcohol Content Statement
Net Contents Statement
Bottler/Importer Name & Address
Government Health Warning Statement
Country of Origin (if imported)

Validation Rules & Pass/Fail Logic
7.1 Brand Name Validation
The label must contain a recognizable brand name.
Passing Conditions
Brand text is clearly readable through OCR
Brand matches the submitted application data (case-insensitive)
Brand appears in the same field of vision as the class/type and alcohol content
Failing Conditions
Brand is missing
OCR confidence is too low to determine brand identity
Brand does not match submitted application data
Brand appears on a separate side of the bottle from alcohol content or class/type designation

7.2 Class/Type Designation Validation
The label must include a valid TTB-recognized distilled spirits classification such as:
Vodka
Whiskey
Rum
Gin
Brandy
Liqueur
Passing Conditions
A valid class/type designation is detected
Text is readable and separate from decorative copy
Appears in the same field of vision as brand name and ABV
Failing Conditions
Missing class/type
Invalid or unrecognized designation
Contradictory classifications
Placement on a different label panel from brand or alcohol statement

7.3 Alcohol Content Statement Validation
TTB requires alcohol content to be displayed as a percentage alcohol by volume.
Examples of valid formats:
“40% Alc. by Vol.”
“15.5% alcohol by volume”
The abbreviation “ABV” alone is not permitted.
Passing Conditions
Contains a numeric percentage
Includes “%”
Includes “Alc.” / “Alcohol”
Includes “Vol.” / “Volume”
OCR confidence meets threshold
Failing Conditions
Uses “40 ABV”
Missing percentage sign
Missing “Alcohol” or “Volume” wording
Numeric value unreadable or malformed
Example
PASS: “40% Alc. by Vol.”
FAIL: “40 ABV”

7.4 Net Contents Validation
TTB requires net contents to be displayed in metric units.
Examples:
“750 mL”
“1.75 L”
Passing Conditions
Numeric value present
Metric unit present (“mL” or “L”)
Formatting readable and properly associated with the numeric value
Failing Conditions
Missing unit
Non-metric-only measurements
OCR unable to identify value
Example
PASS: “750 mL”
FAIL: “750”

7.5 Bottler / Importer Name & Address Validation
The label must include the responsible company name and location following qualifying phrases such as:
“Bottled by”
“Distilled by”
“Imported by”
Passing Conditions
Qualifying phrase detected
Company name present
City and two-letter state abbreviation detected
Text appears together as a single statement
Failing Conditions
Missing qualifier phrase
Missing city or state
Improper formatting
Incomplete address information
Example
PASS: “Bottled by Bright Distillery, Boston, MA”
FAIL: “Bright Distillery Boston”

7.6 Government Warning Statement Validation
The application must detect and validate the federally required Government Warning statement on distilled spirits labels. The system should compare OCR-extracted text against the standard TTB warning language and identify potential mismatches or missing sections.
The required warning statement is:
“GOVERNMENT WARNING: (1) According to the Surgeon General, women should not drink alcoholic beverages during pregnancy because of the risk of birth defects. (2) Consumption of alcoholic beverages impairs your ability to drive a car or operate machinery, and may cause health problems.”
The application should validate:
Presence of the warning statement
Presence of both numbered warning sections
Proper capitalization of “GOVERNMENT WARNING”
Detection of major wording differences or missing text
Passing Conditions
Warning statement is detected
Both required sections are present
Text substantially matches the required wording
OCR confidence is sufficient for validation
Failing Conditions
Warning statement missing
One of the required sections missing
Major wording changes or paraphrasing
Improper capitalization of “GOVERNMENT WARNING”
Because OCR may introduce small recognition errors, minor OCR inconsistencies should be flagged for manual review rather than automatic failure.
Examples
PASS:
Exact required warning statement detected
REVIEW REQUIRED:
Minor OCR typo such as “GeneraI” instead of “General”
FAIL:
Missing pregnancy warning section
“Government Warning” not capitalized
Rewritten or shortened warning text

7.7 Placement & Field-of-Vision Validation
TTB requires the following elements to appear within the same field of vision:
Brand Name
Class/Type
Alcohol Content
The application must use OCR bounding boxes and positional analysis to approximate compliance.
Passing Conditions
Required elements appear visually grouped on the same label panel
Failing Conditions
Brand appears on front label while ABV appears only on back label
Required fields separated across multiple panels

7.8 OCR Confidence & Manual Review Rules
Because OCR errors may occur, the application must include confidence scoring.
System Behavior
OCR confidence ≥ 85% → automatic validation allowed
OCR confidence < 85% → flag for manual review
Critical unreadable fields automatically fail validation
Manual Review Triggers
Ambiguous characters
Partial text extraction
Decorative fonts interfering with OCR
Overlapping label graphics

7.9 Validation Result Categories
Each label must receive one of the following statuses:
Status
Meaning
Pass
All required label elements meet validation requirements
Fail
One or more mandatory TTB requirements violated
Review Required
OCR confidence too low or validation uncertain

The UI should visually identify:
Passed fields (green)
Failed fields (red)
Manual review fields (yellow)
This allows compliance agents to quickly determine why a label passed or failed review.

